<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!-- The Debug half of the same two-file split the Mac app carries, for the same
     measured reason: the App Group entitlement alone makes a teamless macOS
     target refuse to build, and Debug is what a fresh clone builds. Without the
     group this process and the app each fall back to a private directory, so a
     Debug widget honestly renders its "can't share data in this build" empty
     state rather than pretending an App Group exists — see
     `WidgetCache.noDataMessage`, whose whole reason for existing is this
     configuration.

     Two keys the iOS twin carries are absent here on purpose, not forgotten.
     No `com.apple.security.network.client`: widgets never call the PostHog API,
     and an extension that cannot open a socket keeps that rule by construction
     rather than by intention. No keychain group: nothing compiled into this
     target performs a keychain read — the flag toggle records a
     `PendingFlagWrite` in the App Group and opens the app, which owns the
     credential, the rate-limit governor and somewhere to show a 403 (see
     `ToggleFlagFromWidgetIntent`). -->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
